.SS "int32_t schultz_draw_rotation_begin (\fBschultz_draw_list\fP * list, float degrees, float cx, float cy)"

.PP
Appends a rotation, turning everything drawn after it\&. Must be balanced by schultz_draw_rotation_end\&. Rotations nest with each other and with offsets: an offset started inside a rotation moves along the turned axes, which is what makes the two compose the way a person expects\&.
.PP
The angle is in \fBdegrees\fP, clockwise, because y grows downward\&. The centre is a point in the coordinates in force here, so turning a drawing about its own middle is one call rather than an offset either side of it\&.
.PP
This turns what is drawn and nothing else\&. The node keeps its upright bounds, is laid out the same and is hit tested the same\&.
.PP
A clip started inside a rotation is the upright box around the turned rectangle rather than the turned rectangle itself, so it lets through a little more than it names\&. See schultz_draw_clip_begin\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIlist\fP The list to append to\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIdegrees\fP How far to turn, clockwise\&. 
.br
\fIcx\fP The centre to turn about, along x\&. 
.br
\fIcy\fP The centre to turn about, along y\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when list is NULL, or SCHULTZ_ERR_OUT_OF_MEMORY\&. 
.RE
.PP

